 "literal": "Having ploughed, having eaten, those who live indeed, they live; other all, having bowed, having eaten, behind those who go.",
 "translation": "Only those who live by ploughing and eating truly live. All others merely bow, eat, and follow behind.",
 "modern": "நிலத்தை உழுது அதன் மூலம் கிடைக்கும் உணவை உண்டு வாழ்பவர்களே உண்மையாக வாழ்பவர்கள். மற்றவர்கள் அனைவரும் பிறரைத் தொழுது உணவு உண்டு அவர்கள் பின்னால் செல்பவர்களே ஆவர்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet emphasizes the supreme independence and dignity of the farming life. The phrase 'உழுது உண்டு வாழ்வாரே' (those who live by ploughing and eating) highlights that farmers are the only ones who are truly self-reliant. The emphatic 'ஏ' in 'வாழ்வாரே' asserts that theirs is the only true life. In contrast, 'மற்று எல்லாம்' (all others) refers to people in other professions. They must bow ('தொழுது') to others for their livelihood, eating only by following behind ('பின் செல்பவர்') those who produce the food. Thus, agriculture is portrayed as the foundational and most honorable way of life.",
